{"format": "long", "channel": "history", "style": "mapminutes", "voice": "bm_george", "speed": 1.1, "projection": "canada", "id": "mm-nwangle", "title": "Northwest Angle: Why You Drive Through Canada to Get There", "summary": "A 1783 treaty line drawn from a wrong map, patched in 1818, left Minnesota's Northwest Angle reachable by road only through Manitoba. Out west, the 1846 line along the 49th parallel did the same to Point Roberts, Washington.", "tags": ["northwest angle", "point roberts", "minnesota", "lake of the woods", "us canada border", "exclave", "map minutes"], "intro_chapter": "Through Canada to get home", "thumbnail": {"words": ["WRONG", "MAP"], "expr": "surprised", "cam": {"fit": [-97.6, 48.2, -93.6, 50.2], "pad": -0.02}, "layers": {"parallel49": true}, "callout": {"at": [-95.0, 49.27], "arrow_from": [1040, 300], "bend": 50}, "ax": 760, "ay": 380, "fw": 1100, "pin_x": 200, "pin_y": 705, "pin_r": 160, "words_top": 40, "words_right": 50, "words_fs": 130, "needle": 120, "lean": 6}, "sources": ["Treaty of Paris, 3 Sep 1783, Article 2: 'Thence through the said Lake to the most Northwestern Point thereof, & from thence on a due West Course to the River Mississippi' (National Archives, Founders Online): https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Franklin/01-40-02-0356", "Convention of 1818 (signed London, 20 Oct 1818), Article II: a line drawn from the most North Western Point of the Lake of the Woods due north or south, as the case may be, to the 49th parallel, then west along it (Avalon Project, Yale Law School): https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/conv1818.asp", "International Boundary Commission, History: 1783 line to the north-westernmost point of Lake of the Woods and thence due west to the Mississippi; 1818 Article II north or south to the 49th parallel; 1842 Webster-Ashburton Treaty settled the line to the north-westernmost point of Lake of the Woods; 1846 along the 49th parallel to the Strait of Georgia: https://internationalboundarycommission.org/en/about/history.php", "Minnesota Historical Society, MNopedia, Northwest Angle: farther north than any other part of the contiguous United States; the 1783 line followed John Mitchell's map of an egg-shaped Lake of the Woods; in 1798 David Thompson proved the northernmost source of the Mississippi was south of Lake of the Woods; in 1825 Johann Ludwig Tiarks determined the northwesternmost point at the head of Angle Inlet; 1842 treaty confirmed the point: https://mnopedia.org/node/6088", "Minnesota DNR, Mississippi River Headwaters (Itasca State Park): the Mississippi begins at Lake Itasca: https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/state_parks/itasca/headwaters.html", "US Census Bureau, 2020 Census via TIGERweb: Angle township, Lake of the Woods County, MN: population 149, housing units 335, land 323,165,247 m2, water 1,233,153,221 m2 (water = 79% of the area): https://tigerweb.geo.census.gov/arcgis/rest/services/TIGERweb/tigerWMS_Census2020/MapServer/20/query?where=GEOID%3D%272707701550%27&outFields=NAME,GEOID,POP100,HU100,AREALAND,AREAWATER&returnGeometry=false&f=json", "Lake of the Woods Tourism, Northwest Angle: from Warroad MN-313 into Manitoba, 12.6 miles to MB-308, 27 miles to MB-525; the drive goes through Canada for about 40 miles; or a boat across about 40 miles of big water; winter ice trails and groomed snowmobile trails; 'not connected to the rest of Minnesota by land'; 'This is an unmanned border'; check in by resort iPad, ROAM station or the ROAM app; check out of Canada at the Jim's Corner booth or 888-CAN-PASS and write down the verification number: https://lakeofthewoodsmn.com/northwest-angle/", "Lake of the Woods Tourism, Helpful info when driving up to the NW Angle: three CBP kiosks (Jim's Corner, the NW Angle school, Young's Bay); before driving back through Canada call the CBSA from the phone at Jim's Corner, receive a number to write down and keep it in case you are checked: https://lakeofthewoodsmn.com/?p=17678", "Lake of the Woods Tourism, Border information: 'The Northwest Angle has been granted a special use to use the App on smart phones' (CBP ROAM): https://lakeofthewoodsmn.com/border-information", "US Customs and Border Protection, CBP Introduces a New Application for Small Boat Reporting in the Great Lakes: ROAM = Reporting Offsite Arrival - Mobile; 'The CBPO may initiate a video chat to further interview travelers': https://www.cbp.gov/newsroom/local-media-release/new-small-boat-reporting-procedures-now-effect-0", "Canada Border Services Agency, news release 10 Aug 2026: from 1 Dec 2026 travellers in remote areas report at a port of entry or a designated telephone reporting site; areas include the Northwest Angle Area: https://www.canada.ca/en/border-services-agency/news/2026/08/update-permits-extended-until-november-30-2026-cbsa-to-enhance-border-integrity-and-expand-telephone-reporting-in-remote-border-areas.html", "Oregon Treaty, 15 Jun 1846, Article I: the line continues westward along the 49th parallel to the middle of the channel separating the continent from Vancouver's Island (Avalon Project, Yale Law School): https://avalon.law.yale.edu/19th_century/br-1846.asp", "HistoryLink.org Essay 9158, Point Roberts -- Thumbnail History (Phil Dougherty, 2009): a 4.9-square-mile unincorporated American exclave; under the 1846 Oregon Treaty the lower Tsawwassen Peninsula dipped into the US; a 14-mile boat trip from Blaine or a drive nearly twice that far through Canada; the grade school closed in 1963 and students were bused to Blaine, more than 25 miles away; the population more than triples in summer: https://www.historylink.org/file/9158", "US Census Bureau, 2020 Census via TIGERweb: Point Roberts CDP, WA: population 1,191, housing units 2,175, land 12,652,612 m2 (about 4.9 sq mi): https://tigerweb.geo.census.gov/arcgis/rest/services/TIGERweb/tigerWMS_Census2020/MapServer/28/query?where=GEOID%3D%275355015%27&outFields=NAME,GEOID,POP100,HU100,AREALAND,AREAWATER&returnGeometry=false&f=json", "Map data: Natural 